%!PS
% The screen a mark was made under, kept by a record and put back.
%
% A device that renders a grey as a pattern of pixels chooses each pixel
% by the threshold under it, and which thresholds those are is the
% screen in force. That is state a marking call does not carry: the
% device reads it from itself as it paints. So a record that wrote the
% marks down and nothing else would replay them under whichever screen
% its target happened to hold when the page was put out, and a page of
% greys would come back wrong -- while a page of black and white, whose
% pixels sit at the ends of every cell, would come back right and say
% nothing about it.
%
% This paints greys under three screens on one page, and a second page
% that sets none, so the page after a boundary is painted under the
% screen the page before it ended with.
%
%   -DBAND=n       put the page out in bands of n rows' worth of raster
%   -DCOUNT=1      say how many screens the record holds, and quit
%   -DONESCREEN=1  paint the same page under the first screen throughout

/BAND where { pop }{ /BAND 0 def } ifelse
/COUNT where { pop }{ /COUNT 0 def } ifelse
/ONESCREEN where { pop }{ /ONESCREEN 0 def } ifelse

/ID 1183615869 internaldict def

/W 200 def
/H 300 def
<< /PageSize [W H] >> setpagedevice

% The band is stated in rows of the target's raster. A row of this page
% is W samples wherever it is painted, so the budget is rows by width.
BAND 0 gt { DEVICE /.bandbytes BAND W mul put } if

/sA { 60 0 {dup mul exch dup mul add 1 exch sub} setscreen } def
/sB { 133 45 {dup mul exch dup mul add 1 exch sub} setscreen } def
/sC { 20 15 {pop} setscreen } def

% Every grey here is one no cell resolves the same way twice: a grey at
% an end of the range would come out of any screen alike and would hold
% whether or not the screen was carried.
sA
0.5 setgray 10 250 180 40 rectfill
ONESCREEN 0 gt { } { sB } ifelse
0.5 setgray 10 200 180 40 rectfill
0.35 setgray 10 150 180 40 rectfill
ONESCREEN 0 gt { } { sC } ifelse
0.5 setgray 10 100 180 40 rectfill
0.65 setgray 10  50 180 40 rectfill

% more than one kind of mark under the last screen, so what is carried
% is carried for the marks a page makes rather than for rectangles
0.45 setgray
newpath 20 10 moveto 180 10 lineto 100 45 lineto closepath fill
0.6 setgray 2 setlinewidth newpath 20 46 moveto 180 46 lineto stroke

COUNT 0 gt {
    % Asked before the page is put out, which is when the record still
    % holds this page. A device that is not recording has no answer and
    % the run reads the absence.
    DEVICE /.playtargets known {
        (\nSCREENS ) print
        DEVICE ID /.recordscreens get exec 20 string cvs print
        (\n) print
    }{
        (\nNORECORD\n) print
    } ifelse
    flush
    quit
} if

showpage

% The page after a boundary sets no screen of its own. It is painted
% under the one the page before it ended with, which is a screen no
% change on this page announces -- so a record that gave its screens up
% with its marks would paint this page under whatever its target held.
0.5 setgray 10 200 180 60 rectfill
0.6 setgray 10 100 180 60 rectfill
showpage
